{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"**The candidate is not fully consistent with the signed standard.** Its examples mostly express the intended distinctions, but it assigns the comparison with already-open questions to this prompt, while the signed standard assigns that comparison to a later matching step. Its instruction that next year’s figure is “another thesis” also risks splitting a single comparison of two years.\n\nI ran the requested diffs and read the complete candidate system text followed by its closing check. The [c16 schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c16-schema.json) is identical to c8’s.\n\n| Changed text | Verdict against the signed standard | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| “Same thesis or another, judged against the open theses **and the other new theses**” | **Contradicts** in its treatment of open theses; consistent for new theses in the same article. | Section 4 says “这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做” and labels comparison with the existing library “匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的.” |\n| “The same thesis, when the same result settles both: another wording, source, estimate or direction … another population or wording of the same aggregate” | **Consistent** as an identity rule; **contradicts** the standard if this prompt applies it to an already-open thesis. | Section 4 says “同一个结果能同时判两个问题” and lists another estimate, source, wording, direction, population or proxy of the same aggregate. It assigns comparison with the library to matching. |\n| “another form of one plan checked by the same final amount” | **Consistent**, subject to that same division of work. | Section 4 says “同一个计划的几种说法……最后核对的是同一个数，是一题.” An already-signed deal belonging to the plan is merged into the plan’s amount question by matching. |\n| “Evidence, not another thesis: a later deadline for an event while the thesis with the earlier deadline is open” | **Consistent** on the outcome; **contradicts** the standard’s assignment of the comparison to matching. | Section 4 says “早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开.” The complete rule also says that after the earlier deadline expires without a result, the system opens the next deadline with the evidence; this sentence addresses only the earlier question’s open state. |\n| “Another thesis … next year’s figure of a shared quantity, beside this year’s” | **Keeps wider**, if read as an instruction to open both whenever both years appear. | Section 3 allows “自然年的今年或明年” and §4 says the next period of the same quantity is “续期.” But §4 also requires “答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注” and independently significant outcomes. The text includes those tests, yet the concrete “beside this year’s” example can be read more broadly than them. |\n| “whether an unsigned single deal is signed, beside the amount of a programme it may join, when that deal alone could change the company’s revenue, profit, cash or credit risk” | **Consistent**, provided all stated tests are applied. | Section 4 says “还没签的单笔交易是‘签不签’的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题.” Section 3 requires an obligation large enough to change revenue, profit, cash or credit risk. The standard still requires a separate investment judgment, a supported check date and public material. |\n| “One thesis although two years appear: a shared quantity’s change from this year to next is one thesis about next year” | **Consistent** for one change question. | Section 3 gives “2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化” as one example, and says “一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事.” |\n| Closing check: “A new thesis that is the same thesis as an open thesis or another new thesis, **by the rule in section 3**, is not proposed” | **Contradicts** for comparison with an open thesis; consistent for comparison among proposals in the article. | It directs the model back to the new rule, including its comparison with open theses. The signed standard says “和库里的比由匹配做.” This division of work is already present in c8; the changed sentence carries it into the new rule. |\n\nThe standard quotations are from [§3 and §4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:271). The candidate changes are in [c16 system](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c16-system.txt:125) and [c16 closing](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c16-closing.txt:14).\n\n**Other sentences in the request.** There is a real tension within the candidate:\n\n- The new next-year example says “another thesis” for the same quantity “beside this year’s,” while the next bullet says a change from this year to next is **one** thesis. “Beside” does not tell the model whether this year is a baseline in one comparison or an independently tracked outcome.\n- The separate-question rule requires answers that “can move apart” and an analyst who would bet on each. The existing sentence “If the two cannot move apart, link the evidence” agrees with that requirement. A model treating different years alone as sufficient would bypass it.\n- The same-thesis examples agree with “another population or wording of the same aggregate is evidence” and “another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks.” They do not instruct the model to open a question for another estimate.\n- The new deadline and year examples concern question identity. They do not explicitly override “Do not exclude evidence solely because it concerns a related period.” But a model could mistakenly treat separate periods as unrelated and omit a valid evidence link. The existing linking instruction expressly requires it to assess relevance across periods.\n\n**Examples and schema.** The added examples name no specific company, branded product or numeric value, and use several industries. They are industry-neutral in their underlying tests, though some scenarios naturally describe industry-specific activities. The drug and plant examples support the deadline rule; the two forecast and programme examples support the same-question rule; and the two comparison examples support a single year-over-year question.\n\nThe two unsigned-deal examples need facts that the examples do not themselves provide: a check date, a public source that can settle the signing, and enough context to establish the deal’s impact on the company. Calling one “large” alone does not establish that impact. They are illustrative, not complete schema-ready outputs. The schema can represent the questions and their evidence, but its `closest_open_thesis` field only identifies an **open** question; it has no equivalent field for explaining a comparison with another newly proposed question. Thus the new instruction to compare new proposals is expressible in the resulting choice of questions, but that comparison cannot be recorded in the dedicated closest-question field.\n\n**Could it cause the requested failures?**\n\n- **Split a two-year comparison:** yes. The risky words are “**Another thesis … next year’s figure … beside this year’s**.” The later “one thesis” example mitigates this but leaves two competing patterns for a model to choose between. This is a prompt-reading risk, not an observed model failure.\n- **Open a small deal:** the text does not explicitly authorize it. “**when that deal alone could change**” the company’s finances and “**passes the tests above**” protect against it. The example “**one large franchisee’s lease**” could nevertheless encourage a superficial size judgment if read without the company-level impact test.\n- **Open another estimate:** the new same-question bullet expressly groups “**another … estimate**” with the same question, and the existing prohibition reinforces it. The risk arises if “**next year’s figure … beside this year’s**” is treated as a new question merely because an estimate mentions another year, without checking whether it is a separate outcome or evidence for the existing question."}}
